  "notes": "2000s-R&B-and-nu-metal pastiche pop, and the entry says the collision itself is the drum event: \"programmed 2000s R&B kit (tight kick, snappy snare, 16th hats with a swung ghost) colliding with down-tuned guitar-driven double-kick passages; the genre switch is itself the drum event.\" Both halves are authored into one block: the swung ghost is `swing.amount` 0.56 on a 16th grid with a dense four-position ghost map, and the double-kick is the widest `kick.densityPerBar` in this section at 2—8 with a high `offbeat8thShare`. The `crash` and `tomLow` perc lanes are the metal half. The 808 is authored because the entry names it — \"808 sub in the R&B sections; distorted guitar-doubled bass in the metal sections\" — as `counter_riff` so the guitar-doubled bass keeps generating. Harmony is \"4-bar loops alternating maj7/9 R&B voicings with power-chord riffs\" plus \"chromatic and diminished passing harmony borrowed from nu-metal\", so `seventh` and `ninth` together carry more than half the weight and `susOrDimProb` is the highest in this batch. The melody block encodes \"close-stacked in thirds and fifths across whole sections (a wall of Rinas), with a rap-adjacent syllabic verse and a sustained chorus\" as a wide `densityPerBar`, a third-weighted interval spread and a high-density `octave_echo` counter; \"countermelody is a guitar riff\" is the `answer_lick` weight. ⚠ `confidence` is medium and the research says \"tempo/key bands inferred\": every drum probability, `swing.amount`, the humanize numbers and `bpm.mode` 115 (the midpoint of the stated 90—140 band) are INFERRED. ⚠ The entry's [LOW-CONF] mark on the stacked-harmony vocal claim is repeated here.",
